---
import DomainForm from '../components/DomainForm.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Check your SPF, DKIM and DMARC records — notspoofed.com"
  canonicalPath="/"
>
  <h1>Find out why your email is failing authentication</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Enter a domain. We read its public DNS records and tell you exactly what to
    publish to fix what's broken — not just that something is.
  </p>

  <DomainForm autofocus={true} />

  <section class="pitch">
    <h2>What this checks</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>SPF, counted properly</dt>
      <dd>
        SPF permits ten DNS lookups and no more. Past that, receivers give up and
        your record stops working — silently, with no bounce and no warning. We walk
        the whole <code>include:</code> chain, follow <code>redirect=</code>, and
        show you the count. If you're over, we generate a corrected record that
        flattens only the includes it has to.
      </dd>

      <dt>DKIM, without inventing selectors</dt>
      <dd>
        Selectors can't be listed from DNS, so every checker guesses. The catch is
        that some domains answer <em>every</em> DNS query, which makes careless tools
        report keys that don't exist. We verify each hit is genuinely a signing key,
        and tell you plainly that a miss isn't proof DKIM is absent.
      </dd>

      <dt>DMARC, including where your reports actually go</dt>
      <dd>
        We check policy inheritance for subdomains, and whether third-party report
        addresses are authorised to receive your data. That last one catches a
        failure most tools miss entirely: a DMARC record that looks perfect while
        the reports go nowhere.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Why bother</h2>
    <p>
      Since February 2024, Google and Yahoo require SPF, DKIM and DMARC from anyone
      sending them bulk mail. Domains that don't comply get throttled or rejected,
      and the failure is usually invisible from the sending side — your mail simply
      stops arriving.
    </p>

    <h2>Already have a failing message?</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/headers">header analyzer</a> takes the raw headers from a message
      you received and shows the delivery path, where it was delayed, and why DMARC
      passed or failed — including the case where SPF passes but for the wrong domain,
      which is the reason most "we have SPF" domains still fail. It runs entirely in
      your browser; nothing is uploaded.
    </p>

    <h2>Guides</h2>
    <p>
      If you already know what is broken and want to understand it before you change
      DNS, these start from the problem and end with the record:
    </p>
    <ul class="links">
      <li><a href="/guides/spf-too-many-dns-lookups/">SPF: too many DNS lookups</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/dmarc-p-none/">What DMARC p=none actually does</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/dmarc-fails-when-spf-passes/">Why DMARC fails when SPF and DKIM pass</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/dmarc-reports-not-arriving/">Your DMARC reports may be going nowhere</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/google-yahoo-sender-requirements/">Google and Yahoo's sender requirements</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guides/dkim-selectors/">Finding your DKIM selector</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try one of these</h2>
    {/* nofollow: these are the only internal links into /check, and each crawl of a
        result page costs ~48 DNS queries for content we do not want indexed. */}
    <ul class="examples">
      <li><a href="/check?domain=lyft.com" rel="nofollow">lyft.com</a> — SPF over the lookup limit</li>
      <li><a href="/check?domain=paypal.com" rel="nofollow">paypal.com</a> — a fully configured domain</li>
      <li><a href="/check?domain=example.com" rel="nofollow">example.com</a> — wildcard DNS that fools other checkers</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .pitch { margin-top: 56px; }

  dl { margin: 0; }
  dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 24px;
    font-size: 16px;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 7px 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  dd code {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .pitch > p { color: var(--muted); max-width: 68ch; }

  .examples { padding-left: 18px; color: var(--muted); }
  .examples li { margin-bottom: 6px; }

  .links { padding-left: 18px; }
  .links li { margin-bottom: 7px; }
</style>
